T31.01|Direct taxes are imposed on specified income, profit or wealth; indirect taxes apply to transactions or consumption under a legal system. The person remitting a tax and the person bearing its economic burden can differ. Pricing and demand determine how much can be passed on.~~Classification does not determine current rates or eligibility. Tax analysis needs country, residency, period and transaction details.|Direct tax: Liability tied to income or other specified base.;;Indirect tax: Levy on qualifying supplies or transactions.;;Economic incidence: Who ultimately bears the cost.|A seller's pretax price is ₹100 and a hypothetical 10% transaction tax applies.~~Invoice total is ₹110.;;If competition forces total price to remain ₹100, pretax revenue becomes about ₹90.91.;;Assess demand and contractual pricing.~~Remittance and economic burden are different.~~This rate is invented and not a current tax rule.|Identify the taxable base.;;Verify jurisdiction and period.;;Distinguish legal liability from economic burden.
T31.02|Taxable income follows tax law, while accounting income follows the reporting framework. Differences can be permanent or temporary, arising from deductions, recognition timing and exclusions. Accounting profit therefore cannot be multiplied by a headline rate without reconciliation.~~Prepare a bridge and keep cash tax distinct from tax expense. The tax treatment of an item needs legal verification rather than inference from its account name.|Permanent difference: Does not reverse as a timing matter.;;Temporary difference: Reverses through future recovery or settlement.;;Tax reconciliation: Bridges accounting and tax bases.|Assume accounting profit ₹100 lakh, permanently nondeductible expense ₹10 and an extra tax deduction ₹20 that reverses later.~~Taxable profit is ₹90.;;At an invented 25% rate, current tax is ₹22.5.;;Analyse deferred effects separately.~~Taxable and accounting profit differ for identifiable reasons.~~The example assumes the stated legal treatments only.|Reconcile each adjustment.;;Separate permanent and temporary items.;;Verify deductions and recognition rules.
T31.03|A marginal tax rate applies to the next unit of taxable income under the relevant schedule. An effective rate compares total tax with a stated income or profit base. Progressive schedules can produce a marginal rate above the average rate.~~Specify the denominator: effective tax on accounting profit differs from tax as a share of gross income. Credits and special categories can alter both measures.|Marginal rate: Tax on incremental taxable income.;;Average rate: Total tax divided by taxable income.;;Effective accounting rate: Tax expense divided by accounting profit.|Use a fictional schedule: first ₹10 lakh at 10%, next ₹10 at 20%; income is ₹15.~~Tax is ₹1 + ₹1 = ₹2 lakh.;;Average rate is 13.33%.;;Marginal rate on the next rupee is 20% within that band.~~The highest applicable rate is not the rate on all income.~~This is not a real tax schedule.|Use the correct income category.;;State the rate denominator.;;Verify thresholds, credits and dates.
T31.04|Individual and corporate taxes can apply to different legal persons and income categories. A company's retained profit is not automatically an owner's personal taxable income, while distributions or compensation can create additional consequences. Entity choice involves legal, commercial and tax considerations together.~~Compare complete after-tax cash paths rather than one headline rate. Rules on deductions, distributions and ownership differ across systems.|Individual tax: Liability of a person under local rules.;;Corporate tax: Liability of a company.;;Owner-level tax: Consequences of distributions or compensation.|Assume company pretax profit ₹100 lakh, corporate tax 25%, and a hypothetical 20% owner tax on a full distribution.~~Company retains ₹75 after tax.;;Owner-level tax is ₹15.;;Owner receives ₹60.~~Layered tax can produce a different result from either headline rate.~~Real systems may provide exemptions or credits.|Identify the taxpayer.;;Trace cash through every entity.;;Verify credits, deductions and distribution rules.
T31.05|Interest, dividends and capital gains can have different tax character, rates and timing. A capital gain generally depends on proceeds and a legally defined cost basis; a dividend is a distribution under applicable rules; interest compensates lending. Classification should follow the instrument and law.~~Unrealised gains, losses and holding periods may be treated differently. Gross return comparisons should become after-tax cash-flow comparisons.|Interest: Income from lending or deposit terms.;;Dividend: Distribution on ownership claims.;;Capital gain: Change realised under the relevant tax definition.|An asset costs ₹100 and sells for ₹130; assume no costs and a fictional 20% tax on the ₹30 realised gain.~~Tax is ₹6.;;Net sale proceeds are ₹124.;;After-tax gain is 24% of original cost.~~Tax applies to the stated gain base, not necessarily gross proceeds.~~Actual cost basis, timing and exemptions require local rules.|Identify income character.;;Maintain cost-basis records.;;Verify holding-period and loss-offset treatment.
T31.06|Investment-product taxation depends on legal structure, asset exposure, account type and investor status. Similar economic exposure can generate different timing or classification of income. Marketing labels such as long-term or tax-efficient do not establish eligibility.~~Inspect distributions, withholding and sale treatment together. Cross-border funds can introduce additional layers or unrecoverable tax.|Product-level tax: Costs inside the vehicle.;;Investor-level tax: Liability on receipts or disposal.;;Account treatment: Special rules for eligible wrappers.|Two hypothetical funds each earn ₹10 lakh before tax; one loses ₹2 internally to unrecoverable tax and then distributes ₹8.~~Start from the investor's actual ₹8 receipt.;;Apply any assumed investor tax separately.;;Avoid calculating net return from the original ₹10 alone.~~Tax layers can affect tracking and investor outcomes.~~No actual fund regime is asserted here.|Read legal product documents.;;Trace all tax layers.;;Verify eligibility and current local treatment.
T31.07|Tax depreciation and capital allowances determine when eligible investment cost can be deducted. They may differ from accounting depreciation, creating temporary differences. Faster deductions can improve near-term cash tax without increasing the underlying asset's operating productivity.~~Timing benefits should be valued through cash flows. Do not assume all capex is immediately deductible or that allowances apply equally to every asset.|Accounting depreciation: Expense under reporting rules.;;Tax allowance: Deduction permitted by tax law.;;Timing benefit: Earlier deduction changes tax-payment dates.|A ₹100 lakh asset has accounting depreciation ₹20 and an assumed first-year tax deduction ₹40; fictional tax rate is 25%.~~Extra tax deduction is ₹20.;;Current tax is ₹5 lower than under equal deductions.;;Track reversal in later years.~~Accelerated deductions shift tax timing.~~Eligibility and deferred-tax treatment depend on the framework.|Check qualifying expenditure.;;Maintain separate book and tax schedules.;;Model reversal and disposal effects.
T31.08|Loss set-off and carryforward rules allow eligible losses to reduce specified taxable income in the current or future periods. Limits may depend on income category, ownership, time, filing and other conditions. A loss balance is not automatically equivalent to recoverable cash.~~Value depends on future taxable profits and legal usability. Accounting recognition of a related asset must follow the applicable recoverability criteria.|Current set-off: Uses eligible losses against current income.;;Carryforward: Uses losses in future periods.;;Restriction: Limits amount, category or duration.|Assume ₹30 lakh eligible losses and next-year taxable profit ₹20, with full permitted offset and fictional 25% rate.~~Use ₹20 losses.;;Taxable amount becomes zero.;;Remaining losses are ₹10 under these assumptions.~~Tax benefit depends on generating eligible profit.~~Actual caps and expiry may prevent this result.|Verify category and expiry.;;Forecast eligible taxable profits.;;Check ownership and filing conditions.
T31.09|Deferred tax accounts for specified temporary differences between carrying amounts and tax bases. Deferred-tax liabilities often represent future taxable amounts; assets often represent future deductions or eligible losses, subject to recognition rules. They are not current cash taxes.~~Recognition, rates and recoverability follow the reporting framework and enacted or relevant legally supported rules. Permanent differences do not create timing reversals.|DTL: Future tax effect of taxable temporary differences.;;DTA: Recognised future tax benefit of deductible differences.;;Reversal: Recovery or settlement changes the difference.|An asset's carrying amount is ₹80 lakh and tax base ₹60; assume a taxable temporary difference and 25% rate.~~Difference is ₹20.;;Illustrative DTL is ₹5.;;Track changes as the asset is depreciated or sold.~~Book value and tax basis create future tax consequences.~~Exceptions and recognition details depend on the framework.|Reconcile book and tax bases.;;Use appropriate rates.;;Review asset recoverability and reversal timing.
T31.10|GST or other value-added tax systems can create output liabilities and eligible input credits under local rules. Tax cash timing affects working capital even when recoverable amounts do not become final expenses. Delayed collections and restricted credits can create financing needs.~~Do not assume every invoice grants a credit or that tax is due only when cash is collected. Jurisdiction-specific rules determine eligibility and dates.|Output tax: Levy on qualifying sales.;;Input credit: Eligible tax offset on purchases.;;Tax working capital: Cash tied up by payment and recovery timing.|Assume output tax ₹10 lakh and valid available input credit ₹6 in a fictional period.~~Net liability is ₹4 under those assumptions.;;If customers have not paid, the business may still need cash by the statutory due date.;;Model timing and eligibility separately.~~Recoverable tax can still create liquidity pressure.~~No actual filing date or credit rule is implied.|Verify credit eligibility.;;Map tax and collection dates.;;Reconcile returns with invoices and ledgers.
T31.11|Withholding collects tax at payment or credit under specified legal rules. It may be a final tax or an advance credit against a recipient's liability. Gross income, net cash received and ultimate tax expense are therefore distinct.~~Rates, thresholds, certificates and treaty relief depend on the payment and taxpayer. Missing records can delay credit or recovery.|Withholding obligation: Payer deducts and remits specified tax.;;Tax credit: Recipient offsets permitted amount.;;Final withholding: Settles qualifying liability under local rules.|A ₹100,000 payment faces an invented 10% withholding, treated as creditable.~~Recipient receives ₹90,000 cash.;;₹10,000 is remitted as withholding.;;Reconcile the credit with final assessed liability.~~Lower cash receipt does not automatically mean income is ₹90,000.~~Actual timing and documentation require local rules.|Identify payer and recipient duties.;;Keep credit documentation.;;Verify final versus advance treatment.
T31.12|Cross-border tax can involve source-country and residence-country claims on the same income. Treaties may allocate taxing rights or provide relief, subject to eligibility and domestic procedures. Residency, beneficial ownership and permanent-establishment questions are fact-specific.~~A treaty rate should not be assumed from nationality or an offshore account. Verify the relevant treaty, domestic law and documentation.|Source tax: Levy where income arises.;;Residence tax: Levy based on taxpayer residence.;;Double-tax relief: Credit or exemption under applicable rules.|Assume ₹100 income, source tax ₹10 and residence tax ₹25 with a valid full ₹10 credit.~~Residence tax remaining is ₹15.;;Total tax is ₹25.;;Without eligible relief, the cash result could differ.~~Relief prevents double counting only when conditions are met.~~These are fictional rates, not treaty advice.|Determine residency and source.;;Read current primary treaty text.;;Check relief limits and documentation.
T31.13|Transfer pricing evaluates terms of transactions between related parties under applicable tax rules, commonly using an arm's-length principle. It covers goods, services, financing and intangibles. Analysis considers functions performed, assets used and risks borne rather than assigning profit wherever tax is lowest.~~Comparable methods and documentation depend on the transaction and country. Internal management transfer prices may serve different purposes from tax compliance.|Comparable-price method: Uses relevant independent transactions.;;Cost-plus/resale method: Examines margins for defined functions.;;Profit-based method: Tests or allocates profits under permitted approaches.|A related supplier charges ₹120 for an item; comparable independent transactions suggest ₹100 after adjustments.~~Investigate differences in quality, volume and terms.;;Assess the supported arm's-length range.;;Document the chosen method and evidence.~~A raw price gap is a starting point, not a final tax finding.~~Local law and facts govern adjustments.|Map functions, assets and risks.;;Use defensible comparables.;;Keep contemporaneous evidence and local compliance records.
T31.14|Transaction tax depends on whether assets, shares or other rights are transferred and on the jurisdictions and parties involved. Reorganisations may qualify for specific treatment only when conditions are satisfied. Financing deductions, basis changes and indirect taxes can alter deal economics.~~Separate buyer and seller consequences and model the full cash effect. A tax-efficient structure can create commercial or legal disadvantages.|Asset purchase: Transfers specified assets and obligations.;;Share purchase: Transfers ownership of an entity.;;Reorganisation: Changes structure under defined legal terms.|Two fictional structures cost ₹100 lakh; one creates ₹10 present-value tax benefit but adds ₹4 transaction costs.~~Net incremental benefit is ₹6 before other differences.;;Assess transferred liabilities and operating effects.;;Verify eligibility rather than assuming the benefit.~~Structure affects total value beyond headline price.~~No real exemption or deduction is asserted.|Model both parties' cash.;;Review basis and liabilities.;;Verify current transaction-specific rules.
T31.15|Tax planning uses lawful choices and applicable reliefs; evasion involves unlawful concealment or misstatement. Aggressive arrangements may also be challenged under anti-avoidance rules even when their labels sound legitimate. Substance, documentation and disclosure matter.~~The boundary is jurisdiction-specific and should not be inferred from a promoter's claim. A legitimate economic transaction still requires correct reporting.|Lawful planning: Uses available rules with valid facts.;;Evasion: Conceals or falsifies taxable circumstances.;;Anti-avoidance review: Examines arrangements under relevant law.|A taxpayer considers a documented eligible deduction versus fabricating an expense invoice.~~Verify the deduction's actual conditions.;;A fictitious expense misstates facts.;;Seek qualified review for uncertain arrangements.~~Truthful records are fundamental to lawful tax analysis.~~This does not classify every complex arrangement under every legal system.|Verify substance and eligibility.;;Keep accurate records.;;Use qualified advice and primary rules for uncertainty.
T31.16|After-tax valuation discounts cash available after relevant taxes using a consistent required-return framework. Taxes affect operating cash, financing and disposal proceeds. Applying a tax rate to revenue or mixing pretax cash with an after-tax discount rate creates inconsistent results.~~Model timing and jurisdiction explicitly. Hypothetical rates teach mechanics but cannot support an actual investment decision.|After-tax operating cash: Net of relevant operating taxes.;;Financing tax effect: Eligible interest or other deductions.;;Exit tax: Tax on disposal under applicable rules.|An investment pays ₹10 lakh pretax in one year; assume 20% tax on that receipt and 10% after-tax discount rate.~~After-tax cash is ₹8.;;Present value is ₹8 ÷ 1.10 ≈ ₹7.27.;;Compare with cost and other dated flows.~~Tax and discount conventions jointly determine value.~~Loss relief, deductions and capital-gain rules can change real outcomes.|Use consistent cash and rates.;;Model tax timing.;;Verify rules before applying results.
